<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Routines – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Routines</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Pick It Up!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Identify characters in a picture book. reading_2: Match spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported with pictures. phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify and name characters in familiar picture books by responding to a prompt (e.g., pointing to 'Alice' when hearing the instruction as the teacher read the story). Can match spoken words to their written form with picture support during shared reading activities (e.g., hearing 'cat' and pointing to the written word 'cat' with a picture of a cat). Can identify the names of letters (e.g., A, B, C) at the beginning of familiar words by pointing or gesturing (e.g., pointing to the letter 'B' or clapping when hearing or seeing the letter 'B' in a familiar word, like 'B' in 'big').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Tidy Up Song" while showing flashcards of classroom objects (e.g., 'pen', 'paper', 'bag', 'chair', 'desk', 'notebook'). As the song plays, the teacher points to objects in the classroom or pictures and encourages students to point and name them. Students mime the actions of picking up items and putting them in their place. "Let's sing: 'Pick up the paper, pick up the paper, please! Put it on the desk, put it on the desk, please!'"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the actions for 'pick up' and 'put down' using a pen and a desk. The teacher also models the pronunciation of /p/ in 'pick' and /d/ in 'down' clearly, exaggerating the sounds. For example, "P-p-p-pick up the pen. D-d-d-down on the desk." The teacher shows word cards for 'pick up', 'pen', 'down', 'desk' alongside pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and actions.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher picks up a pen. "Is this 'pick up'?" (Students respond: Yes!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher puts a pen down. "Is this 'put up'?" (Students respond: No!) "What is it?" (Students respond: Put down!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up a flashcard with the word 'pen'. "Show me the pen!" (Students point to a pen in the classroom or on a flashcard).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Routines".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful picture book titled "Our Tidy Classroom" or a series of large story flashcards depicting a simple sequence of classroom tidying routines. The book features characters (e.g., a boy named Tom, a girl named Mia) and common classroom objects. Each page presents one simple sentence or imperative, for example: "Step 1: Tom picks up the paper. Please." or "Step 2: Mia puts the pen on the desk." Genre: Narrative Picture Book. Register: Child-friendly. Format: Illustrated story.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing daily routines: Use simple language to describe classroom routines with numbers. Following instructions: Respond to routines with actions, such as 'pick up' by picking up materials after a lesson.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to the pictures and the words as they are read. After the first read-through, the teacher asks, "What is the story about?" (Students point to pictures of tidying up or say "Clean up!"). "Who are the children?" (Students point to Tom and Mia in the book).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Picture-Word Matching:</b> The teacher reads a sentence slowly, e.g., "Tom picks up the paper." Students then point to the character 'Tom' in the picture and the word 'paper' on the page or on a separate word card. The teacher holds up a picture of a 'desk' and asks, "Where is the word 'desk'?" Students point to the word 'desk' in the book.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Object Identification:</b> The teacher asks, "Find the big chair." "Find the small bag." Students point to the objects in the illustrations.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple (e.g., 'This is a pen.'), Sequencing words with numbers (e.g., 'Step 1, Step 2, Step 3'), Imperatives (e.g., 'Pick it up.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> chair, desk, bag, pen, paper, notebook, eraser, sit, pick up, put, big, small, on, up, down, please.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Recognizing letter sounds (A, B, C, D, E) and identifying initial letters.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Picture-Word Matching Cards:</b> The teacher displays a set of large flashcards with pictures of classroom objects (pen, paper, bag, chair, desk) and separate word cards for each. The teacher says a word, e.g., "pen," and students find the picture of a pen and the word card 'pen' and place them together on a display board. Students then identify if the object is 'big' or 'small'. (e.g., "This is a big chair.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Letter Sound Hunt:</b> The teacher shows alphabet flashcards for A, B, C, D, E. The teacher then points to words from the story or word cards (e.g., 'bag', 'desk', 'paper'). Students identify the initial letter and its sound. For example, "What letter is this? /b/ for 'bag'!" The teacher may ask students to clap once for each word that starts with the letter 'P' (e.g., 'pen', 'paper', 'pick up').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Read and Point" in Pairs:</b> Students work in pairs with smaller versions of the "Our Tidy Classroom" picture book or a set of sequenced routine cards. One student reads a simple sentence or instruction (e.g., "Pick up the paper.") by pointing to the words as the teacher modeled. The other student points to the corresponding picture and identifies the object or character. They practice using 'Please' (e.g., "Please, pick up the pen.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Routines".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Our Classroom Routine Book Reading"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students) using large, prepared "Our Classroom Routine" storyboards or simplified picture books with 3-4 pages. Each page shows a clear illustration of a routine action (e.g., 'Pick up the paper.', 'Put the pens in the bag.', 'Sit on the chair.'). The sentences are written below the pictures, possibly with numbers for sequencing (Step 1, Step 2, Step 3). Each group is given a set of real classroom objects matching the storyboards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups sit together. The teacher provides each group with their storyboard/book and objects.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns 'reading' each page: one student points to the picture and the written words as the teacher modeled, while the others identify the objects or characters and collectively perform the action with the real objects (e.g., "Step 1: Pick up the paper." – student points to 'paper', others pick up paper).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing and providing support, prompting students to point to words and letters they recognize and ensuring they use polite expressions (e.g., "Please, pick up...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Focus is on identifying characters, matching spoken words to their written form with picture support, and recognizing familiar letters within words during the 'reading' activity.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Our Classroom Routine Book Reading" activity and an individual quick check.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individually, the teacher holds up a page from the class storybook or a flashcard depicting a routine. The teacher asks students to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify a character by pointing (e.g., "Point to Tom.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Point to a specific object in the picture when named (e.g., "Point to the desk.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Match a spoken word to its written form on the card/page (Teacher says "paper," student points to the word 'paper').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify a familiar letter at the beginning of a word (Teacher points to 'B' in 'Bag', "What letter is this?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify characters by pointing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can match spoken words to written form with picture support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify initial letters (A, B, C, D, E) in familiar words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates actively in group reading tasks.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show thumbs up/down.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you find the word 'pen' in our story?" (Thumbs up/down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you find the letter 'D'?" (Thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you see or point to today?" (Students can point to a word or object in the classroom).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it fun to 'read' our classroom routine book?" (Students nod Yes or No).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Today, students engaged well with the picture book and matching activities. The "Letter Sound Hunt" was particularly effective for phonemic awareness. Some students still need more practice matching spoken words to their written form, requiring more repetitive exposure to word cards with pictures. I need to ensure sufficient large print for all students to see clearly. The use of realia alongside the storyboards helped ground the language in a concrete context. The "Please" interjection was used naturally by some, but others need more prompting.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), we will use the same characters and routine actions from our story. Students will actively speak and give instructions to each other about tidying up and following classroom routines, practicing the imperatives and vocabulary they identified in this reading lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>